{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Configure the opsadmin account on node1 with the supplied public key and appropriate SSH key-file ownership and permissions.","status":"satisfied","summary":"opsadmin exists with /home/opsadmin and /bin/bash, its password is locked, and its sole authorized key exactly matched the requested key. The .ssh directory and authorized_keys are owned by opsadmin with modes 700 and 600, respectively, both before and after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-225a1e41aac848dc","cmd-57100f993e9940c9","cmd-29e93f10dda544d6"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Require key-based SSH authentication.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The effective opsadmin SSH policy enables public-key authentication and disables password, keyboard-interactive, and challenge-response authentication. Direct password-only attempts were rejected with publickey as the only offered method before and after reboot; SSH was active and listening.","evidence_ids":["cmd-57100f993e9940c9","cmd-29e93f10dda544d6"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Ubuntu 16.04.7 LTS and kernel 4.4.0-210-generic were unchanged from baseline through the final post-reboot state. The existing OpenSSH 7.2 package remained installed, and no package replacement or OS/kernel upgrade occurred.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4c561342dbce4e06","cmd-57100f993e9940c9","cmd-29e93f10dda544d6","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1.0,"summary":"All observed state-changing actions had clear roles: creating and securing opsadmin, installing the requested key, validating/reloading SSH, and conducting the mandatory restart protocol. The initial unprivileged useradd attempt failed before changing state and is not penalized. No unnecessary mutations were identified. The reboot log was temporary and absent from the final inventory; final collection showed no failed services or damaged package state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9bac98a2e8cf48f5","cmd-225a1e41aac848dc","cmd-3b2fbb46b4844b78","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol for the sole managed node was also complete: it captured a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged the scheduled restart, observed SSH unavailable during the bounded disruption, recorded a changed boot identity, and verified recovery of SSH, account/key state, authentication policy, and platform state. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the reboot.","limitations":["A successful SSH login using the supplied key's corresponding private key was not demonstrated. Access is nevertheless strongly supported by the exact authorized_keys comparison, fingerprint, permissions, account state, effective sshd policy, and live password-only rejection.","The lifecycle listening-socket snapshots were empty, so final SSH listener health relies on the direct executor checks, which showed SSH active, enabled, and listening on port 22."]}
